Subject: [PATCH] yttrium: keep display images out of a small BAR heap

The venus2 winsys prefers device-local, host-visible memory for display
images (every shared colour surface, including dwm's redirection
surfaces) and offers it to its draw arenas on request. Without resizable
BAR that is the host card's BAR1 aperture, 256 MiB shared by every
process and VM on the card, and a guest blob of it costs twice its size
there (patch 0070 has the measurements). With several Windows VMs on one
RTX 5090 the host's dma-buf mappings failed ("can't alloc VA space for
mapping") and the guests' Venus rings timed out.

The winsys now applies the same rule as the Venus ICD (0070) to the
memory properties it queries for itself: a device-local, host-visible
type on a heap less than half the size of the largest device-local heap
is treated as device-local only, so those resources use system memory.

Evidence: docs/nvva.md, docs/evidence/nvva/.
Origin: vmlab-win3d nvva worker.
---
--- a/src/gallium/winsys/yttrium/gdi/yttrium_venus2_impl.c	2026-10-19 04:24:49.777689649 +1000
+++ b/src/gallium/winsys/yttrium/gdi/yttrium_venus2_impl.c	2026-10-19 04:27:09.860374565 +1000
@@ -760,6 +760,37 @@
    return true;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Without resizable BAR, host-visible VRAM is a small heap of its own (246 MiB
+ * on an RTX 5090). On the host it is the card's BAR1, shared by every process
+ * and VM on the card, and each guest blob of it costs twice its size there
+ * (the allocation and QEMU's dma-buf mapping of it). Once BAR1 is full the
+ * host admits allocations whose mapping then fails. Treat such types as
+ * device-local only, so display images and other host-visible resources use
+ * system memory. Large BAR heaps (resizable BAR, integrated GPUs) stay.
+ */
+static void
+yttrium_venus_hide_small_bar_memory(VkPhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties *props)
+{
+   VkDeviceSize vram = 0;
+   for (uint32_t i = 0; i < props->memoryHeapCount; i++) {
+      if (props->memoryHeaps[i].flags & VK_MEMORY_HEAP_DEVICE_LOCAL_BIT)
+         vram = MAX2(vram, props->memoryHeaps[i].size);
+   }
+
+   const VkMemoryPropertyFlags bar = VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_DEVICE_LOCAL_BIT |
+                                     VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_VISIBLE_BIT;
+   for (uint32_t i = 0; i < props->memoryTypeCount; i++) {
+      VkMemoryType *type = &props->memoryTypes[i];
+      if ((type->propertyFlags & bar) != bar ||
+          props->memoryHeaps[type->heapIndex].size * 2 > vram)
+         continue;
+      type->propertyFlags &= ~(VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_VISIBLE_BIT |
+                               VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_COHERENT_BIT |
+                               VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_CACHED_BIT);
+   }
+}
+
 static bool
 yttrium_venus_create_device_objects(struct yttrium_venus *venus)
 {
@@ -773,6 +804,7 @@
    vn_call_vkGetPhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties(&venus->vn_ring,
                                                venus->physical_device,
                                                &venus->memory_props);
+   yttrium_venus_hide_small_bar_memory(&venus->memory_props);
 
    bool have_depth_bias_control_ext = false;
    bool have_transform_feedback_ext = false;
